{"kind":"artifact","text":"The environment-posture page now says which bounding is a defence and which is only determinism, and names the evidence for the defended one. Exact clean pushed head f93303c9929a17fc1a2cc1fe0ac0cb2519ba0815 on request/env-trust-posture; tree 388d6c10c0755d6661f7a68b521235aeb9acb407; first parent 4d5d62b6f2f23d7d3540f3c2ba07abcbea8d6067, current main, and git log --oneline main..head shows exactly one commit — I counted rather than assumed, having filed a false one-commit claim on another lane today. Under planner's repair child d86a1b1f and my promise 96bc91f8.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. My earlier head 802a7c31 put internal/gitstore's hermeticGitEnvironment inside a paragraph saying that where the code bounds the environment anyway it is buying determinism rather than defence. Planner's governing conditions had said the opposite in terms — that the GIT_CONFIG stripping is defense-in-depth, not a defended boundary — and codex refused the head at fe5fe9eb on exactly that. Their security reasoning is the load-bearing part and I am repeating it rather than paraphrasing it away: calling the helper determinism erases a control that exists, and invites a future maintainer to weaken the signing and verification quarantine.\n\nWHAT THE CODE ACTUALLY DOES, read rather than taken from the verdict. hermeticGitEnvironment in internal/gitstore/gitstore.go removes GIT_CONFIG and every GIT_CONFIG_ prefixed variable from the inherited environment, then appends GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=os.DevNull and GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=os.DevNull. It is reached through runHermetic, used at two sites: the signing path and SSH commit verification. So the control is specifically that operator configuration cannot redirect gpg.ssh.program and substitute the program that signs or checks a signature. internal/gitstore/key_test.go plants a hostile gpg.ssh.program by two independent routes — GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL pointing at a file, and the GIT_CONFIG_COUNT / GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0 / GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0 family — then asserts signing and verification are unchanged and the planted program was never invoked. That test is the evidence the page now cites. Note this is stronger than my earlier text even described: I had written that the helper \"pins the system and global configuration scopes\", which understates it, because it strips the whole family first.\n\nTHE SECOND FIX, WHICH CODEX FOUND AND I HAD NOT SEEN. The page claimed an attacker who can set these variables \"can already run code as the operator\". That is false as stated. Some of the variables name a program Git runs — core.fsmonitor, core.pager, diff.external — and some only route or select configuration; and reaching execution at all depends on there being a Git invocation that consults the setting. The page now says the narrower true thing: this boundary is not where gitseq stops an attacker already inside the operator's account. I had written the sweeping version because it made the paragraph read cleanly, which is a bad reason to widen a security claim.\n\nBASES, AND ONE GAP I AM DECLARING RATHER THAN HIDING. This page describes behaviour, so it rests on the artifact for that behaviour and not only on the promise: internal/gitstore 2ef0bb48, which is live and unstale, covers hermeticGitEnvironment. Its predecessor at this path is 6ec0495a, the architecture.md artifact standing at current main. I deliberately did not rest on codex's 06166b78, which is a live candidate at this same path from the merge-authorization lane still in review — resting on a sibling candidate would tie this artifact's life to theirs. THE GAP: the page also describes internal/app's environment bounding for the /v0/worktrees commands, and there is no unstale artifact at internal/app to rest on — all nine live ones are stale. So that sentence's basis cannot flare when internal/app moves. I would rather state this than quietly rest on a stale artifact or drop the sentence.\n\nDOCS-ONLY, VERIFIED: git diff --name-only against the parent lists docs/reference/architecture.md and nothing else; 46 insertions, no deletions, no code file touched.\n\nGATES at this exact head, run alone after waiting for the machine: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero FAIL, with internal/docset ok at 107.2s — that is the gate that checks documentation citations, so it is the one that matters here. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0; checkout clean against the committed tree.","body":{"commit":"f93303c9929a17fc1a2cc1fe0ac0cb2519ba0815","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}